<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our New Classmates – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Where are you from?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify messages for peers. Text: Summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify messages for peers about school events or activities (e.g., "Victoria said the event will take place at my previous school." "The activity is to learn more about our new classmate Adrian."). Can summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher (e.g., "Oscar talked about his old school. He said it had a big playground and that he loved math class."). Can explain classroom rules to a new student using simple language (e.g., "We can't use our phones during class, but Teacher Angie said we can use them at lunch.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #eaf2f8; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Memory Chain" activity. The teacher starts by saying, "Our new classmate, Andrea, is from Chiriquí. She used to play soccer." The next student summarizes and adds information: "Andrea is from Chiriquí, and she played soccer. Our new classmate, David, is from Colón. He loves science classes." Students continue, summarizing the previous classmate's information and adding a new detail about another "new classmate" (real or imaginary), practicing the present and past simple.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a complex sentence. "In my previous school, which was a very old building in the countryside, we often had really noisy recesses." Teacher rephrases: "My old school was in the countryside. Recess was noisy." The teacher also models how to summarize a short description of a school, highlighting key facts: "My old school had a big library and friendly teachers. It was smaller than this one." Teacher summarizes: "Her old school was small, but it had a big library and kind teachers."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a friend uses many difficult words to explain something, what do you do to understand it better?" (Expected answer: "I ask them to simplify it" or "I try to make it simpler in my head.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I tell you about my old school, and you tell that information to your friend, do you tell them every single word or just the main ideas?" (Expected answer: "Just the main ideas/summarize.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, fictional social media post or chat conversation (approx. 80-100 words) between two students discussing a new classmate's background and favorite subjects, including details about their previous school and hometown. It will contain descriptions using comparatives and present/past simple. (e.g., "Hey, have you met Sofia? She moved from Santiago. Her previous school was smaller but had a really big playground! She told me she used to walk there every day. Now she takes the bus. She also said her old math teacher was stricter than ours. She really likes art, which is interesting!").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Participating in guided discussions about preferences and routines; Sociolinguistic: Presenting personal and classmate information in group discussions using appropriate language.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of the conversation?" (Expected answer: "Talking about a new classmate, Sofia.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False questions:</b> Students decide if statements about Sofia's background are true or false based on the text (e.g., "Sofia's old school was bigger than her new one." (False) "She likes art." (True)).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match details about Sofia (e.g., "Hometown," "Previous school characteristic," "Favorite subject") to the information provided in the text.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying messages and summarizing information using Present and Past Simple for habits and descriptions, Comparatives, and recommended vocabulary (e.g., <i>classmate, school, community, subject, playground, friend, previous, used to</i>). Pronunciation focus on /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings for plurals and third-person singular verbs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Simplify Sentences.</b> Students work individually to rewrite 5-6 longer sentences into shorter, simpler ones, focusing on key information. Example: "Our new friend, Carlos, used to live in a small community near the mountains where he frequently went hiking with his family every weekend." -> "Carlos lived near the mountains. He used to hike on weekends." (Teacher monitors and provides feedback on simplification and correct use of past/present simple. Reinforce /s/ and /z/ sounds in words like "friends" /z/ and "mountains" /z/.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Summarize Classmate Profiles.</b> The teacher provides 2-3 short, fictional "Classmate Profiles" (2-3 sentences each) containing details about their origin, previous school, and hobbies. In pairs, students read a profile and then orally summarize it for their partner in 1-2 simple sentences. (e.g., "Profile: Mariana is from La Chorrera. Her old school was very modern and had a big library. She loves reading books and playing with her friends." Partner summarizes: "Mariana is from La Chorrera. Her previous school was modern with a library. She reads a lot."). Teacher circulates, listening for accurate summaries and correct usage of verbs with /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings (e.g., "loves" /z/, "reads" /z/, "watches" /ɪz/).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Explain Classroom Rules.</b> Students work in pairs. One student pretends to be a new student who doesn't know the classroom rules. The other student explains 2-3 essential classroom rules using simple language, mediating the official rules for their peer. (e.g., "We can't use our phones during class, but Teacher Angie said we can use them at lunch." "We always listen to our teachers and respect our classmates."). Partners then switch roles.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will prepare for the "Our New Classmates Video" project by focusing on the mediation aspect. In groups of three, they will role-play and plan a segment of the video.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format: Group Role-play & Project Planning.</b> Each group identifies one "new classmate" (from previous lessons or an imagined character).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Roles within the group:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interviewer (Student A):</b> Asks questions to the "new classmate" about their background (where they are from, previous school, favorite subjects, hobbies).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simplifier/Narrator (Student B):</b> Listens to the "interview" and then simplifies key information about the new classmate for the video's audience (their peers). This student will use comparatives and superlatives. (e.g., "Liam comes from a bigger school than ours, and he loves science classes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarizer/Explainer (Student C):</b> Listens to the interview and prepares a summary of the new classmate's presentation for a "teacher" or "principal" who might watch the video. This student focuses on concise and polite language, explaining the main points. (e.g., "Oscar talked about his old school. He said it had a big playground and that he loved math class. He will join our football team.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups will present their planned role-play segment to the class, demonstrating how they will mediate information for the video project. Focus is on fluency and accurate mediation, not on actual video production.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will perform their group role-play and planning session for the "Our New Classmates Video" project. The teacher will observe their ability to simplify messages for peers and summarize information for a teacher, integrating the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Formative Rubric/Checklist):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplicity:</b> Simplifies messages for peers effectively using clear, simple language. (Learning Outcome 1)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accurate Summarization:</b> Summarizes information about a classmate's presentation for a teacher, focusing on key details. (Learning Outcome 2)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Uses Present/Past Simple, Comparatives/Superlatives, and vocabulary (e.g., <i>previous, hometown, subject, playground</i>) accurately.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation, including /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates effectively in group discussion and role-play.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to simplify messages by..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was easy/difficult to summarize because..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining or summarizing a new classmate's information was most challenging for you and why?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can mediating information help you in other school subjects or in your daily life?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the mediation activities? Were students able to simplify and summarize effectively?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with the mediation tasks or the project planning? (e.g., more sentence frames, simplified vocabulary lists).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Our New Classmates" theme. The next theme will build on descriptive language and interactions, possibly moving to describing places or future plans related to school activities, consolidating the use of present, past, and future tenses, and applying mediation skills to new contexts.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
</table>